g32\\build32\\Release\\McpXdbg32.dp32` | | Instalado no x32dbg | ✅ `C:\\Reversing\\x32\\plugins\\McpXdbg32.dp32` (o mesmo x32dbg do seu log — db em `C:\\Reversing\\x32\\db`) | | Bridge MCP | ✅ `F:\\PluginXdbg32\\bridge\\mcp_bridge.py` → pipe `\\\\.\\pipe\\McpXdbg32` | ## Como usar (ordem) 1. **Abra o x32dbg** (`C:\\Reversing\\x32\\x32dbg.exe`) → File → Open → `F:\\blackrockmu\\main.exe` 2. O plugin **carrega sozinho** e cria o pipe `\\\\.\\pipe\\McpXdbg32` (só existe enquanto o x32dbg está aberto) 3. O cliente AI (Antigravity/NovaMind) sobe o bridge `python F:/PluginXdbg32/bridge/mcp_bridge.py`, que fala JSON-RPC pelo stdio e encaminha pro pipe 4. Pronto — as 35 ferramentas `xdbg_*` ficam disponíveis ## Pra conectar no seu AI **Antigravity** — merge do `mcp_config_entry.json` em `~/.gemini/antigravity/mcp_config.json`: ```json \"xdbg32\": { \"command\": \"python\", \"args\": [\"F:/PluginXdbg32/bridge/mcp_bridge.py\"] } ``` **Aqui (NovaMind)** — adiciona a mesma entrada no seu config de MCP servers do IDE. Depois eu consigo chamar `xdbg_*` direto. ## Workflow pro seu caso (o BP que você esqueceu) Com o plugin ativo, no x32dbg: ``` bp kernel32.GetStartupInfoA ``` (e ou via ferramenta `xdbg_set_breakpoint`) → **F9** → quando parar: - `xdbg_read_memory` no endereço de `GetStartupInfoA` → ver se o primeiro byte é `E9` (hook do PS.dll) ou `8B` (original) - `xdbg_get_callstack` → ver quem chamou -…\n- **Tools:** list_directory, read_file, glob","vector":[0.046231534,0.093427755,-0.12650448,-0.044064105,-0.014023052,-0.07260805,-0.015634334,0.00823727,-0.030255983,-0.06450427,-0.037106264,0.009843492,0.056794696,-0.024986705,0.043962732,-0.008266546,-0.036100417,-0.0498105,-0.022266006,0.0037692897,-0.025089419,-0.05467548,-0.023963843,-0.009648314,0.09935984,0.04594562,0.04578703,0.04604636,-0.056293365,-0.011420492,0.007908081,0.017986251,0.01013284,-0.02236165,-0.009691159,-0.08911669,0.029508691,-0.02979757,-0.0019080827,0.0039762366,0.022429183,-0.09051454,0.025126152,