,-0.022514913,0.01538677,-0.027158925,0.032997523,0.035132855,0.032326072,0.13105436,0.004468246,-0.0367711,-0.052013077,0.022980515,0.0070179575,-0.024664912,-0.057577733,0.011211204,0.01362634],"hash":"719246d7:1641","model":"nomic-embed-text:latest","archived":true},{"uri":"file:///f%3A/blackrockmu/.novamind/memory/session-log.md","text":"## 2026-11-14T04:46:21.500Z\n- **User:** F:\\MuEmu-master\\eMU\\eMU\\GameServer\\GameServer\\Protocol.cpp avalie o codigo aq, veja o protcolo, pq tem coisa errada...\n- **Outcome:** O dicionário `SkillNames` está **vazio** — por isso as skills aparecem como `Skill 0xXXXX`. Resumo do que encontrei no protocolo do eMU: **Formato real do F3 11 (confirmado no `GCSkillListSend`):** ``` Header: C1 [size] F3 11 [count: 1B] [type: 1B] Entry (4B): [slot: 1B] [m_index: 2B LE] [level_encoded: 1B] level_encoded = (m_level << 3) | ((m_index/255) & 7) → level real = level_encoded >> 3 ``` **Correção aplicada:** `level = data[off+3] >> 3` (antes lia o byte cru) **Agora, para o seu teste com SKILL_FORCE = 60:** O eMU envia `m_index` (índice interno tipo `GET_ITEM(12,x)`), não o skill number. Mas o servidor BlackRock pode usar formato diferente. Para verificar: 1. Rode o proxy e capture o F3 11 2. Veja qual valor de skillId aparece no log 3. Se aparecer `60` → o BlackRock envia o skill number direto 4. Se aparecer outro valor (ex: `0x0C11`) → é o m_index interno Depois é só preencher o dicionário: ```csharp private static readonly Dictionary<ushort, string> SkillNames = new() { [60] = \"Force\", // ... outras skills }; ``` Quer que eu adicione o SKILL_FORCE = 60 no dicionário agora, ou prefere rodar o proxy primeiro para confirmar qual valor o servidor BlackRock realmente envia?\n- **Tools:** search_files, read_file, glob, edit_file","vector":[-0.0021393255,0.029765626,-0.16990581,-0.006895376,0.077435315,-0.049495026,0.04656968,-0.013453862,-0.058270246,0.00033434294,-0.09362234,0.0005278715,0.05051451,-0.03348562,0.040820107,-0.010256506,-0.01