42527124,0.022852607,0.037679028,-0.045067698,-0.0755446,0.028457254,0.0045505133,-0.02736433,0.02577793,0.026038408,0.001137193,-0.0023648026,-0.023491675,-0.019027263,-0.04170244,0.029432578,-0.0055155675,-0.00761754,0.008240883,-0.016562458,-0.07942923,0.030073386,-0.0577154,0.024135383,-0.015356549,0.005972937,-0.0035974619,0.013774341,-0.049687117,0.019570254,0.021808742,-0.0026906675,0.050323784,-0.033885274,-0.031871814,-0.003989747,-0.031057695,0.007434849,-0.0048711468,0.0136290295,0.12971313,-0.010022773,0.04467378,-0.027279299,0.003494117,0.042663552,-0.05654687,-0.047425263,-0.046245895,-0.019834137],"hash":"c56cce1d:540","model":"nomic-embed-text:latest","archived":false},{"uri":"file:///f%3A/blackrockmu/.novamind/memory/session-log.md","text":"## 2026-11-15T20:51:48.492Z\n- **User:** acho que vamos ter mais pacotes que precisarao do xor e eles todos me parecem ter a mesma ideia certo e regra certo oq muda e o SM e envio correto?\n- **Outcome:** Exato — é isso. A regra do XorData é **idêntica para todos os pacotes**: ```cpp // PacketSniffer.cpp:468 — mesma função, mesma regra, auto-inversa for (int n = start; n < end; ++n) data[n] ^= (data[n - 1] ^ g_XorFilter[n % 32]); ``` - Sempre aplica nos bytes `[3, len)` (payload, após `C1 07 XX`) - XOR encadeado: cada byte depende do anterior (anchor = byte 2) - Key fixa `m_XorFilter` (tabela de 32 bytes do update ≥ 601) - É a própria inversa — aplicar duas vezes devolve o plaintext **O que muda por pacote** é só o \"empacotamento final\": | | XorData | SM | Wire | |---|---|---|---| | 0x11 (ataque normal) | ✓ | ✗ | C1 puro | | 0x19 (skill) | ✓ | ✓ (6→11, consome serial `0x08793700`) | C3 0D [11] | | outros C1 futuros | ✓ (mesma regra) | depende do que o builder do jogo fizer | C1 ou C3 | Ou seja: para qualquer pacote novo você sempre parte do **plaintext real**, aplica `GameServerXorData(buf, 3, len)`, e aí decide: vai **C1 puro** (só Layer-1) ou passa por **SM + C3** (lendo o serial de `0x08793700` e incrementando). **Uma ressalva** (já anotada no código, PacketSniffer.cpp:487): o XorData foi **validado por RE/captura só para 0x11 e 0x19**. Para outros opcodes (ex.: movimento) ainda não está confirmado — a regra é a mesma do `PacketManager::XorData` do MuEmu, mas antes de usar em um opcode novo vale validar com uma captura do wire e decifrar de trás para frente (a fórmula do doc `01_xor_decode_and_fix.md`): ``` original[n] = cifrado[n] ^ cifrado[n-1] ^ key[n % 32] ``` Se a deco…\n- **Tools:** read_file, search_files","vector":[0.03156428,0.043816175,-0.13903628,-0.05233583,0.031555563,-0.080922686,0.033758312,0.0012737825,-0.056502536,-0.036274627,-0.0588054,-0.020846937,0.04469065,-0.05494307,-0.0076184557,-0.035479926,-0.010526953,-0.04713313,-0.052325252,0.049937647,0.02352428,-0.01880191,-0.031216728,-0.011040094,0.06598751,0.045637,-0.007603527,0.026688885,-0.072695434,-0.02465413,0.055821106,-0.0068814578,0.018506832,-0.057893235,-0.009747906,-0.05144437,0.044079445,0.0014785815,0.00032969812,0.004317763,0.009492324,-0.06740418,0.014748244,0.0138242915,0.017326586,0.011685723,0.034853335,0.03509644,0.047052197,-0.11771367,0.045509927,0.02935917,0.005039321,-0.041782424,0.06487753,0.029920261,-0.035917968,-0.004021167,0.017857803,-0.048131708,0.08366437,-0.004589851,0.014905654,0.072172366,0.031560313,-0.0048785517,-0.07682013,0.017867114,-0.04709491,-0.012456389,0.07787375,0.014297949,0.019622542,0.037201844,0.026634084,-0.031302106,-0.024281815,-0.013019588,0.016862944,-0.014664631,0.0041143554,-0.03227268,0.076161966,-0.013067091,0.012852854,-0.0030427652,-0.028121976,-0.01804581,-0.04204133,0.086921625,-0.004909069,-0.014855428,0.032752723,0.022149907,-0.041553088,0.0040779435,-0.012366635,0.038037036,-0.023480617,-0.00009571088,-0.045208707,0.01691726,0.043714367,-0.011654319,-0.009960316,0.05089853,0.025065191,-0.0014581564,0.0059606954,-0.0051381686,-0.032193154,0.011000518,-0.035114843,-0.037188537,0.032310475,-0.006869625,0.041099407,-0.030118931,0.030028915,